Fix vmstat_shepherd double-scheduling of a running vmstat_update.

vmstat_shepherd uses delayed_work_pending() to check whether
vmstat_update is already scheduled for a given CPU before queuing it.
delayed_work_pending() only tests WORK_STRUCT_PENDING_BIT, which is
cleared the moment a worker thread picks up the work to execute it.
While vmstat_update is actively running, the shepherd can therefore
queue a second invocation when need_update() is still true, arming a
zero-delay timer for a work that is already executing.  When that timer
expires, delayed_work_timer_fn() -> __queue_work() finds a non-empty
work->entry, warns (kernel/workqueue.c: WARN_ON(!list_empty(&work->entry)))
and drops the work, leaving per-CPU vmstat updates stuck and eventually
hanging the system.

Use work_busy() instead, which covers both WORK_BUSY_PENDING and
WORK_BUSY_RUNNING states.  Upstream fix d725f0664b70 ("mm/vmstat: fix
vmstat_shepherd double-scheduling vmstat_update", 2026-04-08).

diff --git a/mm/vmstat.c b/mm/vmstat.c
--- a/mm/vmstat.c
+++ b/mm/vmstat.c
@@ -2142,7 +2142,7 @@ static void vmstat_shepherd(struct work_struct *w)
 			if (cpu_is_isolated(cpu))
 				continue;
 
-			if (!delayed_work_pending(dw) && need_update(cpu))
+			if (!work_busy(&dw->work) && need_update(cpu))
 				queue_delayed_work_on(cpu, mm_percpu_wq, dw, 0);
 		}
